{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General bias: forecasts are slightly too low during sustained uptrends/rallies (early horizon 1–15d often underpredicts by ~0.3–1.5). Apply a small upward bias when the last 5–10 days show rising levels and higher highs.",
    "Horizon-shape error: the model tends to project a smoother/earlier peak and then too much decline later. True path often stays elevated longer and then mean-reverts less than forecast. Reduce late-horizon (≈20–30d) downward drift.",
    "Overshoot on reversals: after local highs/spikes, the model frequently overestimates the subsequent sell-off (especially visible in examples 3–5 where forecast drops to low 60s while realized stays mid/high 60s). Cap the magnitude of forecasted drawdowns unless the recent history shows accelerating downside momentum.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts too aggressively toward a lower level. Increase the implied long-run anchor toward the recent 10–20d average when the market is range-bound or only mildly correcting.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: upside moves are under-modeled (forecasts lag rallies), while downside moves are over-modeled (forecasts exaggerate declines). Use asymmetric adjustments: boost positive deviations in rising regimes; dampen negative deviations in falling regimes.",
    "Lag/level alignment: forecasts often appear anchored to the pre-rally regime (using older, lower history), creating a 3–10 day lag in level. When a regime shift is detected (break above recent 20–30d range), rebase the entire forecast path upward by the breakout magnitude scaled (e.g., 30–60%)."
  ]
}
